'''Scildism'''<!--Old English of "Shield"--> ([[IPA]]: /ʃi͜yldɪzᵊm/; [[Ottelian language|Ottelian]]: 𐐟𐐷𐐮𐑊𐐼𐐪𐐻, ''Shjyldũt''; [[Qymish language|Qymish]]: Ⱋⱜⰺⰾⱍⰰⱄ, ''Shjylchas'') is a [[wikipedia:Misotheism|misotheistic]] [[wikipedia:syncretism|syncretist]] religion. It has no imposed dogma and no uniformly organized clerical institution. Scildism has its roots in the ancient polytheistic religions of [[Meran]], particularly [[Byrakans|Byrakan]] and [[Ottelians|Ottelian]] traditions. 

The core belief of Scildists is that gods exist, whether they are many or one, and are evil, which consequently makes them enemies of [[wikipedia:Human|humanity]]. In their eyes, it is humanity's duty to protect itself against the gods and their agents among humans. This protection takes the form of human cooperation and ingenuity in the face of disasters, diseases, and inhumane behaviors. The majority of Scildists believe that [[Sluj]] was the [[First hero of Scildism|first hero of humanity]] who recognized the evilness of the gods, although [[First hero of Scildism#Dispute|that view is disputed]]. 

Scildism is currently the third most practiced religion on Basis after [[Sunneism]] and [[Ānism]]. 

==Beliefs==
===Evil gods===
Scildists believe [[wikipedia:misotheism|gods are evil]] and are the enemies of [[wikipedia:human|humanity]]. While they recognize the [[wikipedia:dystheism|capacity of gods to do good]], they assert they only do good from time to time in order to trick humans into lowering their guard and worship them. U/C

===Humanity===
[[wikipedia:Humanity (virtue)|Humanity]] is the most important element of faith in Scildism. U/C

===Apotheosis, divinization and deification===
A central tenet of Scildism is apotheosis, divinization or deification of [[wikipedia:human|humans]], but particularly its [[Corens]]<!--Old English of "Chosen"-->. U/C

==Practices==
===Pilgrimages===
Scildism has a strong tradition of pilgrimages to sites associated with the heroes of humanity. 

The [[Cave of Sluj]] in [[Prasty]], [[County of Prasty]], [[Qym]], is a major site of pilgrimage to honor the [[Byrakans|Byrakan]] hero [[Sluj]]. 

